Article 42 For the following first-instance civil cases, the People's Court may, in accordance with paragraph 1 of Article 39 of the Civil Procedure Law, transfer them to a lower People's Court for trial before the hearing:
(1) Cases concerning the debtor in bankruptcy proceedings;
(2) Cases involving a large number of parties and inconvenience for litigation;
(3) Other types of cases designated by the Supreme People's Court.
Before a People's Court transfers a case to a lower People's Court for trial, it shall report to its superior People's Court for approval. After the superior People's Court approves, the People's Court shall make a ruling to transfer the case to the lower People's Court for trial.

Article 43 In any of the following circumstances, a judicial officer shall withdraw on his/her own initiative, and a party has the right to apply for his/her withdrawal:
(1) He/she is a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) He/she or a close relative of his/hers has an interest in the case;
(3) He/she has served as a witness, expert evaluator, defender, litigation agent, or translator in the case;
(4) He/she is a close relative of a litigation agent in the case;
(5) He/she or a close relative of his/hers holds shares or equity of a non-listed company that is a party to the case;
(6) He/she has any other relationship of interest with a party or a litigation agent in the case that may affect the impartial trial.

Article 44 In any of the following circumstances, a party has the right to apply for the withdrawal of a judicial officer:
(1) He/she accepts banquets from the parties to the case or their representatives, or participates in activities paid for by them;
(2) He/she solicits or accepts property or other benefits from the parties to the case or their representatives;
(3) He/she meets with a party or a litigation agent in the case in violation of regulations;
(4) He/she recommends or introduces a litigation agent to a party in the case, or introduces a lawyer or other person to represent the case;
(5) He/she borrows money or property from a party to the case or their representatives;
(6) He/she engages in any other improper conduct that may affect the impartial trial.

Article 52 The "other organizations" as referred to in Article 51 of the Civil Procedure Law mean organizations that are lawfully established, have a certain organizational structure and property, but do not possess the status of a legal person, including:
(1) Sole proprietorship enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license;
(2) Partnership enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license;
(3) Chinese-foreign contractual joint ventures and foreign-funded enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license in China;
(4) Branches and representative offices of social organizations lawfully established;
(5) Branches of legal persons that are lawfully established and have obtained a business license;
(6) Branches of commercial banks, policy banks, and non-bank financial institutions that are lawfully established and have obtained a business license;
(7) Township enterprises and street enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license;
(8) Other organizations that meet the conditions provided in this Article.

Article 62 In the following circumstances, the actor shall be the party:
(1) Where a legal person or any other organization is required to register but fails to do so, and the actor engages in civil activities in the name of the legal person or the other organization;
(2) Where an actor engages in civil activities in the name of a principal without authority, beyond the scope of authority, or after the termination of authority, unless the counterparty has reason to believe that the actor has authority;
(3) Where a legal person or any other organization is lawfully terminated, but the actor still engages in civil activities in its name.

Article 87 Where, in accordance with Item (3) of Paragraph 2 of Article 61 of the Civil Procedure Law, a relevant social organization recommends a citizen to act as a litigation agent, the following conditions shall be satisfied:
(1) The social organization is a non-profit legal person organization established by registration according to law or exempted from registration according to law;
(2) The principal is a member of the social organization, or the domicile of one party is located within the area of activity of the social organization;
(3) The agency matter falls within the business scope specified in the articles of association of the social organization;
(4) The recommended citizen is the responsible person of the social organization or a staff member who has a lawful labor or personnel relationship with the social organization.
A patent agent recommended by the All-China Patent Attorneys Association may act as a litigation agent in patent dispute cases.

Article 88 In addition to submitting the power of attorney in accordance with Article 62 of the Civil Procedure Law, a litigation agent shall also submit the following relevant materials to the People's Court according to the following provisions:
(1) A lawyer shall submit the lawyer's practicing certificate and the supporting materials from the law firm;
(2) A primary-level legal service worker shall submit the legal service worker's practicing certificate, the letter of introduction issued by the primary-level legal service office, and the supporting materials showing that one party is located within the jurisdiction;
(3) A close relative of a party shall submit the identification document and the supporting materials showing the close relative relationship with the principal;
(4) A staff member of a party shall submit the identification document and the supporting materials showing the lawful labor or personnel relationship with the party;
(5) A citizen recommended by the community or work unit to which the party belongs shall submit the identification document, the recommendation materials, and the supporting materials showing that the party belongs to such community or work unit;
(6) A citizen recommended by a relevant social organization shall submit the identification document and the supporting materials showing that the conditions provided in Article 87 of this Interpretation are satisfied.

Article 91 The People's Court shall determine the burden of proof in accordance with the following principles, unless otherwise provided by law:
(1) The party asserting the existence of a legal relationship shall bear the burden of proof for the basic facts giving rise to such legal relationship;
(2) The party asserting a change, termination, or impairment of a right in a legal relationship shall bear the burden of proof for the basic facts of such change, termination, or impairment.

Article 93 The following facts need not be proved by the parties:
(1) Natural laws, theorems, and laws;
(2) Facts that are generally known;
(3) Facts presumed according to legal provisions;
(4) Facts inferred from known facts and everyday life experience rules;
(5) Facts confirmed by a legally effective judgment or ruling of the People's Court;
(6) Facts confirmed by an effective award of an arbitration institution;
(7) Facts proven by a valid notarized document.
For facts provided in Items (2) through (4) of the preceding paragraph, except where the opposing party has sufficient counter-evidence to rebut; for facts provided in Items (5) through (7), except where the opposing party has sufficient counter-evidence to overturn.

Article 94 The evidence that a party and its litigation agent cannot collect on their own due to objective reasons as provided in Paragraph 2 of Article 67 of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) Evidence kept by a relevant state authority which the party and its litigation agent have no right to inspect or obtain;
(2) Evidence involving state secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy;
(3) Other evidence that the party and its litigation agent cannot collect on their own due to objective reasons.
If the party and its litigation agent cannot collect evidence on their own due to objective reasons, they may apply in writing to the People's Court to investigate and collect it before the expiration of the time limit for producing evidence.

Article 96 The evidence that the People's Court considers necessary for the trial of a case as provided in Paragraph 2 of Article 67 of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) Evidence that may involve damage to national interests or social public interests;
(2) Evidence involving personal status;
(3) Evidence involving an action as provided in Article 58 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) Evidence involving a possibility that a party may collude maliciously to harm the lawful rights and interests of others;
(5) Evidence involving procedural matters such as joinder of parties ex officio, suspension of proceedings, termination of proceedings, or withdrawal.
Except as provided in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall investigate and collect evidence upon a party's application.

Article 111 The circumstances where it is genuinely difficult to produce the original documentary evidence as provided in Article 73 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) The original documentary evidence is lost, destroyed, or damaged;
(2) The original is in the control of the opposing party, and such party refuses to produce it after being lawfully notified to do so;
(3) The original is in the control of another person, and such person has the right not to produce it;
(4) The original is inconvenient to produce due to its length or size;
(5) The party bearing the burden of proof cannot obtain the original documentary evidence through applying to the People's Court for investigation and collection or through other means.
In the circumstances specified in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall, taking into account other evidence and the specific circumstances of the case, examine and determine whether a copy or other reproduction of the documentary evidence may be used as the basis for ascertaining the facts of the case.

Article 166 After a ruling to take preservation measures is made, under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall make a ruling to discharge the preservation:
(1) The preservation is erroneous;
(2) The applicant withdraws the preservation application;
(3) The applicant's prosecution or claim is dismissed by a final judgment;
(4) Other circumstances under which the People's Court considers that the preservation shall be discharged.
Where preservation measures implemented by registration are discharged, a notice of assistance in enforcement shall be issued to the registration authority.

Article 170 The urgent circumstances specified in Item 3 of Article 109 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) The need to immediately stop the infringement or remove the obstruction;
(2) The need to immediately prevent a certain act;
(3) The recovery of insurance claim money urgently needed for resuming production or business operation;
(4) The need to immediately return social insurance funds or social assistance funds;
(5) The failure to immediately return the money would seriously affect the right holder's life and production or business operation.

Article 176 Where any participant in the proceedings or any other person commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 113 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) making audio recordings, video recordings, or photographs without permission;
(2) broadcasting trial activities on-site by means of mobile communication or otherwise without permission;
(3) other acts that disrupt court order and obstruct the conduct of trial activities.
Where the circumstances set forth in the preceding paragraph exist, the People's Court may temporarily seize the equipment used by the participant in the proceedings or other person for making audio recordings, video recordings, photographs, or broadcasting trial activities, and order the deletion of the relevant content; if the person refuses to delete the content, the People's Court may take necessary measures to compel such deletion.

Article 187 The act of obstructing judicial officers in performing their duties by violence, threats, or other means as prescribed in Article 114, paragraph 1, item 5 of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) making a din or staying in the People's Court without following the dissuasion of judicial officers;
(2) intentionally damaging or seizing legal documents or seizure marks of the People's Court;
(3) making a din or storming the scene of official duties, surrounding or detaining officers performing or assisting in official duties;
(4) damaging, seizing, or detaining case materials, official vehicles, other official equipment, uniforms of officers performing official duties, and official credentials;
(5) obstructing judicial officers from inquiring, sealing, seizing, freezing, transferring, auctioning, or selling property by violence, threats, or other means;
(6) other acts of obstructing judicial officers in performing their duties by violence, threats, or other means.

Article 188 The act of refusing to perform a judgment or ruling of the People's Court that has become legally effective as prescribed in Article 114, paragraph 1, item 6 of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) concealing, transferring, selling off, or damaging property, or gratuitously transferring property, trading property at a clearly unreasonable price, waiving due debts, or gratuitously providing security for others after the legal instrument has become legally effective, thereby rendering the People's Court unable to enforce the instrument;
(2) concealing, transferring, damaging, or disposing of property that has been provided as security to the People's Court without the court's permission;
(3) engaging in consumption in violation of the People's Court's order restricting high consumption;
(4) having the ability to perform but refusing to perform the obligations determined by an effective legal instrument in accordance with the enforcement notice of the People's Court;
(5) an individual who has the obligation to assist in enforcement refuses to assist in enforcement after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court.

Article 189 Where any participant in the proceedings or any other person commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 114 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) impersonating another person to initiate or participate in litigation;
(2) a witness, after signing an undertaking, gives false testimony, hindering the People's Court in trying the case;
(3) forging, concealing, destroying, or refusing to hand over important evidence concerning the judgment debtor's ability to perform, hindering the People's Court in ascertaining the property status of the judgment debtor;
(4) unauthorizedly unfreezing property that has been frozen by the People's Court;
(5) after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, tipping off the parties and assisting them in transferring or concealing property.

Article 192 Where, after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, a relevant entity commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 117 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) allowing the judgment debtor to engage in high consumption;
(2) allowing the judgment debtor to leave the country;
(3) refusing to stop handling procedures such as the transfer of property right certificates, registration of ownership changes, and planning approvals;
(4) delaying the handling on grounds such as the need for internal consultation, internal approval, or internal regulations.

Article 216 Before the first court session of the People's Court, if the defendant raises an objection to the acceptance of the civil case on the ground that there is a written arbitration agreement, the People's Court shall review the objection.
Upon review, if any of the following circumstances is met, the People's Court shall rule to dismiss the prosecution:
(1) an arbitration institution or a People's Court has already confirmed the validity of the arbitration agreement;
(2) the parties did not raise an objection to the validity of the arbitration agreement before the first court session of the arbitral tribunal;
(3) the arbitration agreement complies with Article 16 of the Arbitration Law and does not have the circumstances specified in Article 17 of the Arbitration Law.

Article 225 Depending on the specific circumstances of the case, the pretrial conference may include the following:
(1) clarifying the plaintiff's claims and the defendant's defense opinions;
(2) reviewing and handling applications by parties to add or change claims, and any counterclaim filed, as well as claims related to the case raised by a third party;
(3) deciding, upon a party's application, to investigate and collect evidence, to commission expert evaluation, to require the parties to provide evidence, to conduct inspections, and to preserve evidence;
(4) organizing the exchange of evidence;
(5) summarizing the issues in dispute;
(6) conducting mediation.

Article 247 Where a party files another lawsuit regarding a matter for which a lawsuit has already been instituted, either during the course of the proceedings or after the judgment has taken effect, and the following conditions are concurrently satisfied, it shall constitute a duplicative prosecution:
(1) The parties in the subsequent action are identical to those in the prior action;
(2) The subject matter of the subsequent action is identical to that of the prior action;
(3) The claims in the subsequent action are identical to those in the prior action, or the claims in the subsequent action substantially negate the result of the prior judgment.
If a party institutes a duplicative prosecution, the People's Court shall rule not to accept the case; if already accepted, the People's Court shall rule to dismiss the prosecution, unless otherwise provided by law or judicial interpretation.

Article 252 In a case where a retrial ruling sets aside the original judgment or ruling and remands the case for retrial, if a party applies to change or add claims or raises a counterclaim, and any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall permit it:
(1) The original trial, without lawful summons, entered a default judgment, affecting the party's exercise of litigation rights;
(2) A new litigant is added;
(3) The subject matter of the action is lost or has changed, rendering the original claims impossible to realize;
(4) The claims changed or added by the party or the counterclaim raised by the party cannot be resolved through a separate lawsuit.

Article 255 For an application to inspect a judgment or ruling, the People's Court shall handle it according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the judgment or ruling has already been made public through the information network, the court shall guide the applicant to inspect it on their own;
(2) If the judgment or ruling has not been made public through the information network, and the application meets the requirements, the court shall promptly provide convenient inspection services;
(3) If the judgment or ruling has not yet taken legal effect, or has ceased to have legal effect, the court shall not provide inspection and shall inform the applicant;
(4) If the judgment or ruling that has taken legal effect was not rendered by this court, the court shall inform the applicant to apply for inspection with the People's Court that rendered the effective judgment or ruling;
(5) If the content sought to be inspected involves state secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy, the court shall not permit the inspection and shall inform the applicant.

Article 257 The summary procedure shall not apply to the following cases:
(1) Where the defendant's whereabouts are unknown at the time of prosecution;
(2) Where the case is remanded for retrial;
(3) Where one party has a large number of persons;
(4) Where the trial supervision procedure applies;
(5) Where state interests or public interests are involved;
(6) Where a third party initiates a lawsuit requesting the alteration or revocation of an effective judgment, ruling, or mediation document;
(7) Other cases to which the summary procedure is not suitable to apply.

Article 263 When trying a case under the summary procedure, the case file shall contain the following materials:
(1) The statement of claim or the record of oral prosecution;
(2) The statement of defense or the record of oral defense;
(3) The identity certification documents of the parties;
(4) The power of attorney for authorizing another person to act as an agent ad litem, or the record of oral authorization;
(5) Evidence;
(6) The record of questioning the parties;
(7) The record of trial (including mediation);
(8) The judgment, ruling, mediation document, or mediation agreement;
(9) The record of service and pronouncement of judgment;
(10) The status of enforcement;
(11) The receipt for litigation costs;
(12) If the case is tried in accordance with Article 165 of the Civil Procedure Law, the written notice concerning the application of the procedure.

Article 270 In a case tried under the summary procedure, if any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court may appropriately simplify the part concerning the findings of facts or the reasons for the judgment when preparing the judgment, ruling, or mediation document:
(1) The parties have reached a mediation agreement and a civil mediation document needs to be prepared;
(2) One party expressly admits all or part of the claims of the other party;
(3) In a case involving trade secrets or personal privacy, one party requests simplification of the relevant content in the judgment document, and the People's Court considers the reason to be justified;
(4) Both parties agree to simplification.

Article 282 Where an authority or relevant organization as provided for by laws such as the Environmental Protection Law and the Law on the Protection of Consumer Rights and Interests initiates a public interest action under Article 58 of the Civil Procedure Law against conduct that pollutes the environment, infringes upon the legitimate rights and interests of numerous consumers, or otherwise harms the social public interest, the People's Court shall accept the case if the following conditions are met:
(1) there is a definite defendant;
(2) there are specific claims;
(3) there is preliminary evidence that the social public interest has been harmed; and
(4) the case falls within the scope of civil actions accepted by the People's Court and under the jurisdiction of the court receiving the action.

Article 293 The phrase "did not participate in the action due to reasons not attributable to itself" as provided in Article 59, paragraph 3 of the Civil Procedure Law means the situation where the person was not listed as a party to the effective judgment, ruling, or mediation document and is not at fault or has no obvious fault. It includes:
(1) not knowing of the action and thus not participating;
(2) applying to participate but not being permitted;
(3) knowing of the action but being unable to participate due to objective reasons; or
(4) not participating due to other reasons not attributable to itself.

Article 295 The People's Court shall not accept an action for setting aside by a third party brought against any of the following circumstances:
(1) a case handled under non-contentious procedures such as special procedures, supervision procedures, public summons procedures, or bankruptcy procedures;
(2) the part of a judgment, ruling, or mediation document concerning invalidity of marriage, annulment of marriage, or dissolution of marital relationship, which involves status relations;
(3) an effective judgment or ruling in a representative action case with respect to a right holder who did not register to participate as provided in Article 57 of the Civil Procedure Law; or
(4) an effective judgment or ruling in a public interest action case with respect to a victim of conduct harming the social public interest as provided in Article 58 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 298 With respect to a third party's request to set aside or partially set aside the content of a legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation document, the People's Court shall, after trial, handle the matter according to the following circumstances separately:
(1) if the request is established and the claim for confirmation of its civil rights is wholly or partially established, the erroneous part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation document shall be altered;
(2) if the request is established but the claim for confirmation of all or part of its civil rights is not established, or no request for confirmation of its civil rights is made, the erroneous part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation document shall be set aside;
(3) if the request is not established, the claim shall be dismissed.
If a party is not satisfied with the judgment or ruling under the preceding paragraph, it may appeal.
The part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation document that is not altered or set aside shall remain effective.

Article 300 Where the third party's claims are merged into the retrial procedure for trial, they shall be handled according to the following circumstances separately:
(1) if the retrial is conducted under the first instance procedure, the People's Court shall try the third party's claims together, and the judgment rendered may be appealed;
(2) if the retrial is conducted under the second instance procedure, the People's Court may mediate; if mediation fails to reach an agreement, it shall rule to set aside the original judgment, ruling, or mediation document and remand the case to the first instance court for retrial, and the third party shall be listed in the retrial.

Article 311 With respect to an action on enforcement objection initiated by the applicant for enforcement, the People's Court shall, after trial, handle the matter according to the following circumstances separately:
(1) if the person not involved in the case does not have a civil right or interest in the subject matter of enforcement sufficient to preclude compulsory enforcement, it shall be adjudged that enforcement of the subject matter of enforcement is permitted;
(2) if the person not involved in the case has such a right or interest, the claim shall be dismissed.

Article 317 Where one or some of the necessary co-litigants file an appeal, the matter shall be handled according to the following circumstances separately:
(1) if the appeal only concerns the apportionment of rights and obligations between the appellant and the other party, and does not involve the interests of other co-litigants, the other party shall be the respondent, and the same-side parties who did not appeal shall be listed according to their procedural status in the original trial;
(2) if the appeal only concerns the apportionment of rights and obligations among co-litigants, and does not involve the interests of the other party, the same-side parties who did not appeal shall be the respondents, and the other party shall be listed according to its procedural status in the original trial;
(3) if the appeal concerns the apportionment of rights and obligations between both parties and among co-litigants, all other parties who did not file an appeal shall be respondents.

Article 323 The following circumstances may be identified as a serious violation of statutory procedure as provided in Article 177, paragraph 1, item (4) of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) the composition of the adjudicatory body is unlawful;
(2) a judicial officer who should have withdrawn did not withdraw;
(3) a person with no capacity for litigation was not represented by a legal representative in the action; or
(4) a party's right to debate was illegally deprived.

Article 331 The People's Court of the second instance may, in accordance with Article 176 of the Civil Procedure Law, hear the following appeal cases without holding a hearing:
(1) appeals against rulings on non-acceptance, jurisdictional objection, or dismissal of the action;
(2) the appeal claims raised by the parties are obviously unsustainable;
(3) the facts found in the original judgment or ruling are clear, but the application of law is erroneous; or
(4) the original judgment seriously violates statutory procedure and needs to be remanded for retrial.

Article 345 The public notice for locating the missing person shall include the following:
(1) The respondent shall report his or her specific address and contact information to the accepting court within the prescribed period. Otherwise, the respondent shall be declared missing or declared dead;
(2) Any person who knows the current living status of the respondent shall report the information known to the accepting court within the period of the public notice.

Article 355 Where a party applies for judicial confirmation of a mediation agreement, if any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall make a ruling not to accept the application:
(1) It does not fall within the scope of acceptance by the People's Court;
(2) It does not fall within the jurisdiction of the People's Court receiving the application;
(3) The application seeks confirmation of invalidity, validity, or dissolution of status relationships such as marital relationship, parent-child relationship, or adoptive relationship;
(4) It involves cases to be heard under other special procedures, public summons procedure, or bankruptcy procedure;
(5) The content of the mediation agreement involves confirmation of real rights or intellectual property rights.
After accepting the application, the People's Court finds that there is any of the above circumstances for non-acceptance, it shall make a ruling to dismiss the party's application.

Article 358 Upon examination, if the mediation agreement has any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall make a ruling to dismiss the application:
(1) It violates mandatory provisions of laws;
(2) It harms national interests, public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others;
(3) It violates public order and good customs;
(4) It violates the principle of voluntariness;
(5) Its content is unclear;
(6) Other circumstances where judicial confirmation cannot be made.

Article 365 An application for realization of a security interest shall be accompanied by the following materials:
(1) A written application, which shall state the basic information such as the name or title and contact information of the applicant and the respondent, the specific request, and the facts and reasons;
(2) Materials proving the existence of the security interest, including the principal contract, the security contract, the mortgage registration certificate or certificate of other rights, the certificate of rights for a pledge of rights or the registration certificate of the pledge, etc.;
(3) Materials proving that the conditions for realization of the security interest have been satisfied;
(4) A statement on the current status of the secured property;
(5) Other materials that the People's Court considers necessary to submit.

Article 370 Upon examination, the People's Court shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the parties have no substantial dispute over the realization of the security interest and the conditions for realizing the security interest are satisfied, a ruling shall be made to permit the auction or sale of the secured property;
(2) If the parties have a partial substantial dispute over the realization of the security interest, a ruling may be made to permit the auction or sale of the secured property with respect to the undisputed part;
(3) If the parties have a substantial dispute over the realization of the security interest, a ruling shall be made to dismiss the application, and the applicant shall be informed to file an action with the People's Court.

Article 375 A party applying for retrial shall submit the following materials:
(1) A written application for retrial, along with copies equal to the number of the respondent and other parties in the original trial;
(2) If the applicant for retrial is a natural person, a certificate of identity shall be submitted; if the applicant for retrial is a legal person or other organization, the business license, the organization code certificate, and the certificate of the legal representative or the principal responsible person's identity shall be submitted. If another person is entrusted to file the application, a power of attorney and the agent's identity certificate shall be submitted;
(3) The judgment, ruling, or mediation statement of the original trial;
(4) The main evidence reflecting the basic facts of the case and other materials.
The materials specified in items (2), (3), and (4) of the preceding paragraph may be copies verified as identical to the originals.

Article 376 The written application for retrial shall state the following:
(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original trial;
(2) The name of the People's Court of the original trial and the case number of the original adjudicative document;
(3) The specific request for retrial;
(4) The statutory grounds for applying for retrial and the specific facts and reasons.
The written application for retrial shall clearly indicate the People's Court to which the application is made, and shall be signed, fingerprinted, or sealed by the applicant for retrial.

Article 381 If a party applies for retrial under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall not accept the application:
(1) The application is made again after the application for retrial has been dismissed;
(2) The application is made against a judgment or ruling rendered in the retrial;
(3) The application is made after the People's Procuratorate has made a decision not to file a retrial procuratorial recommendation or protest against the party's application.
For the circumstances specified in items (1) and (2) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall inform the party that it may apply to the People's Procuratorate for a retrial procuratorial recommendation or protest, except where the judgment or ruling was rendered in a retrial that resulted from the People's Procuratorate's issuance of a retrial procuratorial recommendation or protest.

Article 386 If the applicant for retrial proves that the new evidence it submits meets any of the following circumstances, the reasons for late submission of evidence may be deemed to be established:
(1) It existed before the conclusion of the original trial hearing, but was discovered after the hearing due to objective reasons;
(2) It was discovered before the conclusion of the original trial hearing, but could not be obtained due to objective reasons or could not be provided within the prescribed time limit;
(3) It was formed after the conclusion of the original trial hearing, and a separate action cannot be filed based on it.
If the evidence submitted by the applicant for retrial was already provided in the original trial, and the original trial People's Court did not organize cross-examination and did not use it as a basis for adjudication, the reasons for late submission of evidence shall be deemed to be established, except where the original trial People's Court decided not to admit the evidence in accordance with Article 68 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 388 Any of the following circumstances, which leads to an erroneous result in the judgment or ruling, shall be deemed as the original judgment or ruling having erroneously applied the law as specified in Article 207(6) of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The applied law is clearly inconsistent with the nature of the case;
(2) The determination of civil liability clearly violates the agreement of the parties or the provisions of the law;
(3) A law that has ceased to have effect or has not yet come into force is applied;
(4) Provisions on the retroactive effect of law are violated;
(5) Rules on the application of law are violated;
(6) The legislative intent is clearly violated.

Article 389 Any of the following circumstances during the original trial hearing shall be deemed as depriving the party of the right to debate as specified in Article 207(9) of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The party is not allowed to express its arguments;
(2) The case should have been heard in an open hearing but was not;
(3) The service of a copy of the statement of claim or a copy of the appeal brief violates the legal provisions, thereby causing the party unable to exercise its right to debate;
(4) Other circumstances where the party's right to debate is illegally deprived.

Article 391 The legal instruments referred to in Item (12) of Article 207 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) judgments, rulings, and mediation documents that have become legally effective;
(2) arbitration awards that have become legally effective;
(3) notarized instruments of creditor's rights that are effective for compulsory enforcement.

Article 400 During the review of an application for retrial, if any of the following circumstances exists, a ruling shall be made to terminate the review:
(1) the applicant for retrial dies or is terminated, and there is no successor to rights and obligations, or the successor renounces the application for retrial;
(2) in an action for performance, the respondent obligated to perform dies or is terminated, and there is no property available for enforcement and no person who shall assume the obligation;
(3) the parties reach a settlement agreement and have performed it in full, except where the parties declare in the settlement agreement that they do not waive the right to apply for retrial;
(4) a person without authorization applies for retrial in the name of a party;
(5) the court of original trial or the higher People's Court has already ruled to retry the case;
(6) the circumstance provided in paragraph 1 of Article 381 of this Interpretation exists.

Article 402 When conducting a hearing in a retrial case, the People's Court shall proceed in the following order:
(1) For retrial initiated by a party's application, the applicant for retrial shall first state the requests and grounds for retrial, followed by the respondent's defense and the opinions of other parties in the original trial;
(2) For retrial initiated by a protest, the protesting authority shall first read the protest letter, then the party that applied for the protest shall make a statement, followed by the respondent's defense and the opinions of other parties in the original trial;
(3) For retrial initiated ex officio by the People's Court where there is a petitioner, the petitioner shall first state the requests and grounds for retrial, followed by the respondent's defense and the opinions of other parties in the original trial;
(4) For retrial initiated ex officio by the People's Court where there is no petitioner, the original plaintiff or the original appellant shall first make a statement, followed by the opinions of other parties in the original trial.
For the circumstances set forth in items (1) to (3) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall require the parties to clarify their requests for retrial.

Article 404 During the retrial proceedings, if any of the following circumstances exists, a ruling may be made to terminate the retrial procedure:
(1) the applicant for retrial withdraws the request for retrial during the retrial and the People's Court grants the withdrawal;
(2) the applicant for retrial, after being summoned by service of summons, refuses to appear in court without justifiable cause, or leaves the courtroom without permission during the hearing, and is treated as having withdrawn the request for retrial;
(3) the People's Procuratorate withdraws its protest;
(4) the circumstances provided in items (1) to (4) of Article 400 of this Interpretation exist.
For a case where a retrial is ordered due to a protest filed by the People's Procuratorate, if the party that applied for the protest is in any of the circumstances set forth in the preceding paragraph and the national interest, social public interest, or lawful rights and interests of others are not harmed, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the retrial procedure.
After the termination of the retrial procedure, the enforcement of the original effective judgment that was suspended by the People's Court's ruling shall be automatically resumed.

Article 414 Where a local People's Procuratorate, at the request of a party, files a retrial procuratorial recommendation with the People's Court at the same level against an effective judgment or ruling, the People's Court shall accept it if the following conditions are satisfied:
(1) the retrial procuratorial recommendation, the application of the party in the original trial, and relevant evidentiary materials have been submitted;
(2) the object of the recommended retrial is a judgment or ruling that may be retried under the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) the retrial procuratorial recommendation specifies that the judgment or ruling falls under the circumstances provided in Paragraph 2 of Article 215 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) the circumstances provided in Items (1) and (2) of Paragraph 1 of Article 216 of the Civil Procedure Law are satisfied;
(5) the retrial procuratorial recommendation has been discussed and decided by the Procuratorial Committee of that People's Procuratorate.
If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw the recommendation; if it fails to do so, the People's Court shall notify the People's Procuratorate in writing that the recommendation is not accepted.

Article 415 Where the People's Procuratorate, at the request of a party, files a protest against an effective judgment or ruling, the People's Court shall, within thirty days, rule to retry the case if the following conditions are satisfied:
(1) the protest letter, the application of the party in the original trial, and relevant evidentiary materials have been submitted;
(2) the object of the protest is a judgment or ruling that may be retried under the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) the protest letter specifies that the judgment or ruling falls under the circumstances provided in Paragraph 1 of Article 215 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) the circumstances provided in Items (1) and (2) of Paragraph 1 of Article 216 of the Civil Procedure Law are satisfied.
If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw the protest; if it fails to do so, the People's Court may rule not to accept the protest.

Article 427 Where a creditor applies for an order for payment and meets the following conditions, the Primary People's Court shall accept the application and notify the creditor within five days from the date of receiving the application:
(1) the claim is for payment of money or negotiable instruments such as bills of exchange, promissory notes, checks, stocks, bonds, treasury bonds, transferable certificates of deposit, etc.;
(2) the money or negotiable instruments claimed have matured and the amount is definite, and the facts and evidence on which the claim is based are stated;
(3) the creditor has no counter-obligation of performance;
(4) the debtor is within the territory of China and has not disappeared or his whereabouts are unknown;
(5) the order for payment can be served on the debtor;
(6) the People's Court that receives the application has jurisdiction;
(7) the creditor has not applied to the People's Court for pre-action preservation.
If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court shall, within five days from the date of receiving the application for an order for payment, notify the creditor that the application is not accepted.
The acceptance of an application for an order for payment by a Primary People's Court shall not be limited by the amount of the creditor's claim.

Article 428 After accepting the application, the People's Court shall have one judge review the case. Upon review, if any of the following circumstances exists, a ruling shall be made to dismiss the application:
(1) the applicant does not have the status of a party;
(2) the documentary evidence for payment of money or negotiable instruments does not provide for interest on overdue payment or liquidated damages or compensation, but the creditor insists on claiming such interest, liquidated damages, or compensation;
(3) the money or negotiable instruments claimed are illegal gains;
(4) the money or negotiable instruments claimed have not yet matured or the amount is not definite.
If, after accepting the application for an order for payment, the People's Court finds that the conditions for acceptance as prescribed by this Interpretation are not met, it shall rule to dismiss the application within fifteen days from the date of acceptance.

Article 430 Where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the procedure for hastening debt recovery, and if an order for payment has been issued, the order for payment shall automatically become invalid:
(1) after the People's Court accepts the application for an order for payment, the creditor files a lawsuit on the same creditor-debtor relationship;
(2) the order for payment cannot be served on the debtor within thirty days from the date of issuance;
(3) the creditor withdraws the application before the debtor receives the order for payment.

Article 435 Upon formal examination, if a written objection raised by the debtor falls under any of the following circumstances, the objection shall be deemed established, a ruling shall be made to terminate the procedure for hastening debt recovery, and the order for payment shall automatically become invalid:
(1) the circumstances for non-acceptance of the application as provided in this Interpretation;
(2) the circumstances for dismissal of the application by ruling as provided in this Interpretation;
(3) the circumstances where a ruling to terminate the procedure for hastening debt recovery shall be made as provided in this Interpretation;
(4) the People's Court has reasonable doubt as to whether the conditions for issuing an order for payment are satisfied.

Article 445 The public notice of acceptance of an application issued by the People's Court in accordance with Article 226 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China shall specify the following:
(1) the name of the applicant for public notice for assertion of claims;
(2) the type, number, face amount, drawer, endorser, holder, payment term and other particulars of the instrument, as well as the type, number, scope of rights, right holder, obligor, exercise date and other particulars of other right instruments for which a public notice for assertion of claims may be applied;
(3) the period for asserting rights;
(4) the legal consequences of transferring the instrument or other right instruments during the period of public notice for assertion of claims if the interested person fails to assert rights.

Article 458 The justifiable reasons referred to in Article 230 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China include:
(1) the interested person was unable to know the facts of the public notice due to an accident or force majeure;
(2) the interested person was unable to know the facts of the public notice because his/her personal freedom was restricted, or although he/she knew the facts of the public notice, was unable to assert rights personally or by an authorized agent;
(3) the circumstances do not fall within the statutory conditions for applying for public notice for assertion of claims;
(4) the public notice was not issued or was not issued in the statutory manner;
(5) other objective reasons that prevented the interested person from asserting rights to the People's Court before the judgment was rendered.

Article 478 A notarized document of a creditor's right as referred to in Article 245, paragraph 2 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China may be determined to be indeed erroneous under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the notarized document of a creditor's right is a document of a creditor's right that shall not be given the effect of compulsory enforcement;
(2) the judgment debtor party did not appear in person or authorize an agent to appear for notarization, or there are other serious violations of the statutory notarization procedures;
(3) the content of the notarized document of a creditor's right is inconsistent with the facts or violates mandatory provisions of law;
(4) the notarized document of a creditor's right does not state that the judgment debtor agrees to accept compulsory enforcement if it fails to perform or fails to fully perform its obligations.
If the People's Court determines that enforcement of such notarized document of a creditor's right is contrary to social public interests, it shall issue an order not to enforce it.
After the notarized document of a creditor's right is ordered not to be enforced, the parties and interested persons of the notarization matter may initiate an action regarding the dispute over the creditor's right.

Article 520 The People's Court may identify a civil case as a foreign-related civil case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) one or both parties are foreigners, stateless persons, foreign enterprises or organizations;
(2) the habitual residence of one or both parties is outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(3) the subject matter is outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(4) the legal facts that give rise to, change or extinguish the civil legal relationship occur outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(5) other circumstances under which the case may be identified as a foreign-related civil case.

Article 530 Where a foreign-related civil case meets all of the following conditions, the People's Court may rule to dismiss the plaintiff's lawsuit and inform it to file an action with a more convenient foreign court:
(1) the defendant requests that the case be subject to the jurisdiction of a more convenient foreign court, or raises a jurisdictional objection;
(2) there is no agreement between the parties choosing the jurisdiction of a court of the People's Republic of China;
(3) the case does not fall under the exclusive jurisdiction of the courts of the People's Republic of China;
(4) the case does not involve the interests of the state, citizens, legal persons or other organizations of the People's Republic of China;
(5) the main facts of the dispute do not occur within the territory of the People's Republic of China, and the case is not subject to the law of the People's Republic of China, and there are significant difficulties for the People's Court in finding facts and applying law in adjudicating the case;
(6) a foreign court has jurisdiction over the case and it is more convenient for that court to adjudicate the case.
